"A deployment diagram maps software instances or artifacts onto execution nodes in a particular environment.\n\nUse when: Explain where applications run and how deployed instances connect. Review environment-specific placement and infrastructure boundaries.\n\nAvoid when: The purpose is only logical responsibility decomposition.\n\nVisual grammar: Nested nodes identify physical devices, virtual machines, or execution environments. Artifacts or container instances sit within the nodes that host them. Communication paths name protocols and relevant infrastructure relationships.\n\nBuild: Choose one environment and state whether it is observed or proposed. Identify execution nodes and their nested environments. Place software instances and shared infrastructure on the correct nodes. Pad nested boundaries and route communication paths through open corridors between nodes.\n\nCheck: Logical applications are distinguished from their deployed replicas. Environment and observation date are explicit. Artifact placement agrees with verified deployment evidence or labeled design assumptions. Node titles and hosted-instance labels remain clear of paths.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing staging and production into one unexplained deployment. Assuming a logical container corresponds to exactly one machine.\n\nExample: Production places two Booking API instances behind a load balancer and a separate database service in the selected region.\n\nAlternatives: Container diagram, Replication topology."
